# World 2's ship-deck winch, a sloppy approach (SPEC 11, "Winch and loading polish", v1.43). Ctrl+L, W, 5 starts mission 5 at the base pad; skill 1 is pinned
# after the world has loaded (no wind, no jets) and ghost mode is on. tests/w2deck.agk flies to the exact spot (press left 771); here the stick is let
# go six frames early (3 px a frame at cruise: the chopper comes to rest about 18 px past slice C's 24 px box, which is outside it, and inside skill 1's
# 56 px box): with slice C's rules the winch never started ("a little klugey"), with the box of v1.43 it does, the hook falls, and people load one at a
# time: boards at 1 s intervals, the group on deck thins. The unit tests (testW2Winch*, testW2Deck*) hold the rules.
wait-serial "AGK t0" 1000
key 0x63
key 0x28
wait 5
key 0x11
wait 5
key 0x05
wait-serial "AGK world 2 loaded" 700
key 0x63
key 0x28
wait 5
key 0x01
wait 30
key 0x63
key 0x28
wait 5
key 0x24
wait 30
press fire 2
wait 60
press up 60
press left 765
press down 45
wait 30
wait-serial "AGK winch start" 300
wait 30
screenshot runs
wait-serial "AGK winch board=1" 200
wait 2
screenshot board1
wait-serial "AGK winch board=2" 200
wait 25
screenshot climb2
wait-serial "AGK winch board=3" 200
wait 2
screenshot board3
expect-serial "AGK winch start[\s\S]*AGK winch board=1[\s\S]*AGK winch board=2[\s\S]*AGK winch board=3"
expect-no-serial "AGK ERR|missing"
expect-no-dropped-frames
# (a new scenario: the sibling world 2 scenarios assert 98; measured 92-97, none dropped)
expect-max-load 98
expect-min-chip-free 96
